A generation can be written as doubles through pointers to doubles

opn2_generateFormat() takes the buffers it writes as OPN2_UInt8 *, whatever the format, and its documentation says so: the buffers must be cast into bytes arrays. For OPNMIDI_SampleType_F64 the library casts each address back to double * and stores the sample through it, so a program that keeps its samples as doubles has to hand its double * over as a pointer to bytes -- a reinterpret_cast in its own code, however it is spelt -- for the library to turn back into what it was.

opn2_generateDouble() writes the same samples, those opn2_generateFormat() writes for OPNMIDI_SampleType_F64 and converted by opn2_cvtReal<double>() as they are, through pointers to double: sampleCount / 2 of them to each of left and right, stride samples apart. The generation loop moves out of opn2_generateFormat() into a function the two calls share, which is told where the samples go, so opn2_generateFormat() does what it did, and the new sender copies what SendStereoAudio() copies, from where it copies it. It returns 0 when either buffer is missing, rather than taking the target for one in bytes. The code is C++98, as the library is.

ADLplug-Next's players ask for binary64 samples and keep them as doubles on their way to the host; with this they hand the library their buffers as the doubles they are. libADLMIDI gets the same call from patches/libADLMIDI/0009.

This is ADLplug-Next's own patch, applied to the submodule by cmake/Patches.cmake when CMake configures, and it goes away once the change is upstream.

diff --git a/include/opnmidi.h b/include/opnmidi.h
--- a/include/opnmidi.h
+++ b/include/opnmidi.h
@@ -1039,6 +1039,27 @@ extern OPNMIDI_DECLSPEC int  opn2_generate(struct OPN2_MIDIPlayer *device, int s
  */
 extern OPNMIDI_DECLSPEC int  opn2_generateFormat(struct OPN2_MIDIPlayer *device, int sampleCount, OPN2_UInt8 *left, OPN2_UInt8 *right, const struct OPNMIDI_AudioFormat *format);
 
+/**
+ * @brief Generate PCM stereo audio output as binary64 samples without iteration of MIDI timers
+ *
+ * What opn2_generateFormat() writes for OPNMIDI_SampleType_F64, written through pointers to
+ * doubles rather than through the bytes of the buffers, so that the buffers need not be cast
+ * into bytes arrays. Each of @p left and @p right takes sampleCount / 2 samples, @p stride
+ * samples apart: 1 for a buffer of its own for each channel, 2 for one buffer of interleaved
+ * frames with @p right one sample after @p left.
+ *
+ * Don't use count of frames, use instead count of samples. One frame is two samples.
+ * So, for example, if you want to take 10 frames, you must to request amount of 20 samples!
+ *
+ * @param device Instance of the library
+ * @param sampleCount
+ * @param left Left channel buffer output
+ * @param right Right channel buffer output
+ * @param stride Distance between two samples of a channel, in samples
+ * @return Count of given samples, otherwise, 0 or when catching an error while playing
+ */
+extern OPNMIDI_DECLSPEC int  opn2_generateDouble(struct OPN2_MIDIPlayer *device, int sampleCount, double *left, double *right, unsigned stride);
+
 /**
  * @brief Periodic tick handler.
  * @param device
diff --git a/src/opnmidi.cpp b/src/opnmidi.cpp
--- a/src/opnmidi.cpp
+++ b/src/opnmidi.cpp
@@ -1169,6 +1169,34 @@ static int SendStereoAudio(int         samples_requested,
     return 0;
 }
 
+static int SendStereoDouble(int        samples_requested,
+                            ssize_t    in_size,
+                            int32_t   *_in,
+                            ssize_t    out_pos,
+                            double    *left,
+                            double    *right,
+                            size_t     stride)
+{
+    if(!in_size)
+        return 0;
+
+    size_t outputOffset = static_cast<size_t>(out_pos);
+    size_t inSamples    = static_cast<size_t>(in_size * 2);
+    size_t maxSamples   = static_cast<size_t>(samples_requested) - outputOffset;
+    size_t toCopy       = std::min(maxSamples, inSamples);
+
+    left  += (outputOffset / 2) * stride;
+    right += (outputOffset / 2) * stride;
+
+    for(size_t i = 0; i < toCopy / 2; ++i)
+    {
+        left[i * stride] = opn2_cvtReal<double>(_in[2 * i]);
+        right[i * stride] = opn2_cvtReal<double>(_in[(2 * i) + 1]);
+    }
+
+    return 0;
+}
+
 
